FitView 1.0 - NativeCall PRIVATE TEST BUILD
===========================================

THIS IS A PRIVATE TEST BUILD. Do not pass it on, upload it or put it in any
archive: it contains code from the NativeCall SDK (ncsupport.c, and the
module loader's conventions), which has no licence yet (all rights reserved
by its author). The public build is the FitView in Utilities.lha (no
NativeCall).

What it is
----------

The same FitView (same arguments, menus, keys and settings), built for the
68040 with a NativeCall module (fitview.narm, embedded) that runs its pixel
work as AArch64 code on the Raspberry Pi under Emu68:

  hpass    the high-quality scale across (WBFit's bicubic, widened when
           shrinking; four channels) (C)
  vpass    the high-quality scale down, 16 bytes at a time (NEON)
  nn       the fast preview while the window changes (nearest neighbour)
  fill     the background around the picture
  comp     pictures with alpha over the checkerboard
  dither   the window's picture to the screen's pens (nearest colour or
           Floyd-Steinberg), screens of 8 bits or fewer

Reading the picture (datatypes, in FitView's loader process), the window,
the menus and drawing to the screen (WritePixelArray, WriteChunkyPixels)
stay on the 68k. Each native call stops after about 0.3 ms (the ARM
counter) and is called again from where it stopped, and a row of a kernel
is never more than about 32768 filter taps (wide rows are done in column
chunks), so the window answers between calls; one ARM core.

This is what makes live resizing smooth on a big RTG screen: the preview and
the refined picture of a 1920x1080 window come from the ARM in a few
milliseconds instead of seconds on the 68k.

Every kernel gives the same bytes as the 68k code (tested under
qemu-aarch64_be with the module itself, NEON included: random rows, taps,
weights, palettes, alpha, pens, and whole windows of every zoom mode, 32-bit
and palette pictures, at the sizes of real use, compared byte for byte). All
the arithmetic is integer, so native and 68k results are identical, not
just close.

It falls back to the 68k code when there is no nativecall.library, no
NativeCall armstub, no 68040/68060, or a buffer native code can't see. If a
native call ever fails (NC_ST_FAULT or a wrong range), that band is drawn
again with the 68k code and native calls stay off for the rest of the run.

Trying it
---------

  Copy FitView C:FitView-nc       (or anywhere; it is one file)

  FitView-nc Pics:photo.jpg BENCH
  FitView-nc Docs:manual.pdf

BENCH draws the window's picture once the first view is refined, then
again with the 68k code, and prints (in the Shell; from Workbench in a
requester) the preview and the refined scale, native against 68k, with
whether the pixels are the same, and each kernel's native calls (the
hypervisor's times: calls, total, average and longest call; "!" marks a
call over 1 ms). The Settings menu's "Benchmark" does the same at any time.

The About requester (Help key, or Project/About) says whether the kernels
run natively and, if not, why.
